(version 1)

# GENERATED by scripts/gen_netclasses.py -- do not edit.
#
# Vendor land patterns.  A clearance constraint governs what the LAYOUT may do; it has no
# business policing the gap between a part's own pads, which is fixed by the manufacturer's
# recommended footprint and is identical on every board that uses the part.  The exposed
# thermal pad of a VSON/QFN/HSOF sits 0.20 mm from its neighbouring signal pads, and U10's
# land pattern is tighter still at 0.15 mm -- both under this board's 0.25 mm global minimum
# and under the POWER class's 0.30 mm.  No edit to this board can change that; the only real
# choices are to use the part or not to use it.
#
# Suppressing the check is therefore correct, but it is NOT free: it moves the requirement out
# of DRC and into the fab notes.  Each ref below is listed with the gap it actually needs --
# down to 0.15 mm -- and the board is no easier to build than its tightest entry.  The
# assembler likewise needs a stencil and reflow profile that will not bridge an exposed
# thermal pad to the pin 0.15-0.20 mm away.  Say so on the fab drawing.
#
# The list comes from scanning the generated board for intra-footprint pad pairs on DIFFERENT
# nets closer than the clearance being applied, so it cannot drift out of step with the
# schematic.  A rule keyed on a ref that no longer exists would be silently dead; a part that
# newly needs one appears here on the next run.
#
# The condition has to name each ref.  A.memberOfFootprint('U1') works; the tempting
# A.memberOfFootprint(B) parses, never matches, and fails open with no error at all.

# Ground-plane pour necks, GND_IN1 / GND_IN3 / GND_IN6 only.
#
# min_connection is 0.2000 mm and these pours hold four necks of 0.1558 to 0.1845 mm, repeated
# identically on all three layers because those zones are plane-only -- no track segment on any
# of them -- and all 1405 vias are full F.Cu-B.Cu barrels, so the three planes see exactly the
# same antipad field.
#
# min_connection asks for the narrowest copper that may carry a CONNECTION, i.e. a series path.
# A neck between two antipads in a solid plane is not a series path: the plane is a sheet and
# current spreads around the constriction through the copper beside it.  It would only matter
# if cutting it stranded copper, and it does not: KiCad reports 0 isolated_copper (now at
# `error`, not `warning`), scripts/check_zone_islands.py reports no zone unconnected to itself,
# and the only unconnected GND item on the board is U4.4 -- the one pad gen_pcb warns has no
# room for a drop at all.  140 of the 141 SMD GND pads carry their own full-barrel drop via, so
# each of the four GND pours is tied to the others at 140 points.
#
# What remains to ask of a plane neck is whether it ETCHES, so this is set at the vendor floor
# and not at zero: 0.10 mm is PCBWay's inner-layer minimum at 1 oz (4 mil = 0.1016 mm), the
# figure that chose 1 oz inners in scripts/gen_netclasses.py's module docstring.  The narrowest
# neck here is 0.1558 mm, 1.5x that.
#
# Scoped to these zones by name and to A.Type == 'Zone', deliberately: a global relaxation
# would also stop this rule catching a router track meeting a pad across its rounded corner,
# which is what it caught on F.Cu at U10_DRN, Q10_G and Net-(Q12-B).
(rule "ground plane pour necks"
	(constraint connection_width (min 0.10mm))
	(condition "A.Type == 'Zone' && (A.Name == 'GND_IN1' || A.Name == 'GND_IN3' || A.Name == 'GND_IN6')"))

# U1 needs 0.200 mm pad-to-pad
(rule "land pattern U1"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.12mm))
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('U1')"))

# U2 needs 0.200 mm pad-to-pad
(rule "land pattern U2"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.12mm))
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('U2')"))

# U10 needs 0.150 mm pad-to-pad
(rule "land pattern U10"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.12mm))
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('U10')"))
